/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (Imagine an agent maintaining beliefs about a changing world. An observation can lead it to revise those\012beliefs. An action can change the world or reveal information about it. Some outcomes matter more than\012others, so preferences belong in the model as well. Active inference brings those pieces into a common\012framework for inference and policy selection. The proposal compares policies by expected free energy,\012with preferred consequences and information value under an explicit probability model. Prediction-error\012learning can be one component, but it does not by itself supply the whole loop.\012\012In our setting, someone supplies a correction. The system can store it and decide whether to use it when\012a new question arrives. A future agent might additionally decide which question to test next: another\012paraphrase, a nearby fact, or an ordinary-text context where a correction could do damage. That action\012would buy information at some cost. The proposed policy would need an explicit probability model,\012preferences, and a way to compare information value with consequences. We have not implemented that\012policy. The experiments I am about to show provide a place to ask whether it would be useful.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
