/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (A language model maps the text so far into probabilities for the next token. Its internal activations\012pass through a sequence of transformer blocks. We keep the main model's learned weights fixed, and\012place an adaptive correction system, which we call a cap, around a few of those internal sites.\012\012The cap stores records for the supplied facts. A trained reader compares the current query with memory,\012and a gate can choose no correction at all. When a record is selected, bounded residual vectors are added\012at the write sites. The model then continues computing its next-token distribution. A residual is simply\012an added correction to an existing activation; we are not replacing the entire transformer.\012\012The attraction is a separation between a stable prior and adaptable memory. The difficulty is that a\012small local intervention can still have consequences downstream. Freezing the weights prevents one kind\012of change; it does not guarantee unchanged predictions. The diagrams in our abstract call these two\012interfaces blankets. Here they are software interfaces; the required statistical blanket properties have\012not been demonstrated.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
