/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (An edit begins with a prompt and the answer we want the system to produce. First we ask whether it can\012produce that answer immediately. After a long stream of other edits, we ask the original prompt again.\012That tests whether the memory survived. Then we ask a paraphrase, a differently worded version of the\012same request. That tests whether the system can find and use the memory outside the exact teaching\012prompt. Paraphrase retention is the main outcome in the central study.\012\012We also ask about preservation. Unrelated prompts should keep their old behavior. Nearby prompts are\012more challenging because they resemble an edited question while referring to another fact. Revisions\012test whether the newer answer replaces an older one. Finally, ordinary text lets us compare token\012probabilities even when a visible answer does not change. These tests will often disagree, and that\012disagreement is part of the result rather than a nuisance to average away.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
