/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (CounterFact asks a slightly different question. We deliberately teach a counterfactual target, so the\012target is an experimental instruction, not a claim about the world. In the first example we teach\012Spanish for James Howell, where the dataset lists English as the previously true answer. The paraphrase\012includes an unrelated prefix sentence, yet the learned cap produces Spanish.\012\012The second example teaches Philippines for the origin of Gol and Gincu The Series, where the stored\012previously true answer is Malaysia. At the endpoint, the original prompt still gets Philippines. But the\012paraphrase receives the displayed free continuation instead. The saved selection flag says that the\012learned cap did not fire on this paraphrase. We can therefore describe the observed routing failure\012without guessing from the final score alone.\012\012The contrast is useful because both memories look successful if we only repeat the teaching prompt.\012The paraphrase reveals the difference. It also reminds us that these are strict bounded-answer scores:\012producing fluent text is not the same as giving the requested experimental target.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
