/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (Here is a nearby-fact test. We teach New Zealand for the displayed Frits Poelman prompt, then ask the\012similar question about George Pitt Morison. The base's bounded answer is empty. The learned reader\012keeps it empty; the random reader instead supplies New Zealand, the supplied target for the other\012question. A third condition produces Paris. The dataset's stored neighboring answer is\012Australia, but preservation is scored against the base's output, not against that stored factual answer.\012\012The same issue is especially clear in an unrelated locality prompt about Fred Flintstone's wife. The\012base outputs a question mark and the learned cap also outputs a question mark. That passes preservation;\012it is plainly not a demonstration of answering the question correctly. This is why I will not call a\012perfect locality score a guarantee of safe or knowledgeable behavior.\012\012Pause for about twenty seconds: does the actual input-and-output example make the correction task clearer\012than the architecture diagram? A quick show of hands is enough; save longer reactions for the discussion.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
