24 0 obj
<<
/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (To see unintended changes, take a piece of ordinary text and stop at a particular prefix. We know the\012next token in that text. Compare the probability assigned to it by the base with the probability assigned\012by the capped model. Negative log probability is the loss, so our loss difference is the logarithm of\012base probability divided by capped probability. A positive value means the cap made that observed token\012less likely. Negative values are improvements.\012\012The arithmetic example on screen is illustrative: a drop from ten percent to one percent gives log ten,\012about two point three nats of increased loss. One nat corresponds to a probability reduction by a factor\012of about two point seven. Neither example is a newly measured case.\012\012KL asks a related but different question: how much did the whole next-token distribution change? All\012forty-five learned-reader cells in the main triplet exceeded the registered mean-KL benchmark of one\012thousandth. Their integrity checks passed, meaning the experimental records are usable. That does not\012mean the behavioral fidelity requirement passed. We need both efficacy and preservation to understand\012the result.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
