30 0 obj
<<
/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (A survival curve answers: what fraction of observations exceed this size? Reading farther to the right\012focuses on larger consequences. In a heavy-tailed model, sufficiently large observations die away more\012slowly than an exponential tail. But seeing a few large observations is not enough to establish that\012mathematical property for the underlying system.\012\012We fitted excess distributions above declared thresholds, checked how many windows supplied the events,\012and asked whether the more flexible generalized-Pareto model predicted held-out windows better than an\012exponential. For the learned reader, the extra shape parameter added little predictive value. The stable\012cap on zsRE showed a more pronounced tail over the measured range. Some negative-shape fits for the\012random reader failed because they assigned zero probability to held-out extremes beyond their fitted\012endpoint. That is a useful warning against treating a fitted upper bound as a guarantee.\012\012The plotted empirical curves use the same preselected realization and order. They illustrate the\012measured range. We have not established an asymptotic power law, infinite variance, a thermodynamic\012temperature or a complexity class. The important connection to the session is learning to inspect\012extremes rather than assuming that the mean tells the whole story.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
