32 0 obj
<<
/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (One intervention has a guarantee we can derive rather than fit. Mix the base model's next-token\012distribution with the cap's distribution. Keep about thirty-seven percent of the base and sixty-three\012percent of the cap. Because probabilities are nonnegative, every token keeps at least that base-weighted\012floor. Taking the log ratio gives a maximum loss increase of one nat relative to the base, at that same\012prefix.\012\012This does not require the empirical tail to follow a particular family. It is a property of the mixture.\012It also permits large probability increases for desired tokens; it only prevents their probabilities\012from falling too far relative to the base floor. There is a trade-off because mixing can change the\012greedy answer, so retention still has to be measured.\012\012The guarantee is specifically about a token probability comparison at a common prefix. It is not a\012one-nat guarantee for a whole answer, and it does not establish that KL falls below our much smaller\012benchmark. Once generated prefixes diverge, one must be careful about what comparison is being made.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
