37 0 obj
<<
/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (Now to the learning rule. A taught target creates a prediction loss. We need a signal saying which\012internal correction would reduce that loss. The adjoint path computes a derivative through the model,\012then uses the negative normalized direction. This is our comparison condition.\012\012In the error-inference path, we introduce temporary error variables at the write sites. Start them at\012zero. Hold the existing memory writes fixed while those error variables settle. The energy balances a\012quadratic penalty on the inferred errors against the task loss produced with those errors present.\012After a finite number of optimization steps, the inferred site errors supply directions for the same\012bounded memory update used by the comparison arm. Our default is eight settling steps at step size\012zero point one.\012\012That is the predictive-coding component we tested. There are different predictive-coding formulations;\012this implementation uses automatic differentiation through the computation graph. It is not evidence\012for biologically local learning or an implementation free of backpropagation. The scientific question\012is whether this particular iterative credit rule buys more useful correction, less harm, or a better\012trade-off for its cost. Those are measured questions, not consequences of calling the method predictive\012coding. Separately, we also trained the reader itself using error inference; I will distinguish that\012experiment from changing acquisition credit.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
