44 0 obj
<<
/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (We next moved the acquisition-credit comparison onto the working GPT-2 reader. The base, trained reader,\012gate, memory capacity and teaching streams stayed fixed; each arm acquired a fresh memory. At three\012hundred edits, zsRE paraphrase retention is unchanged. CounterFact falls by about a third of a percentage\012point with error credit. The positive-harm expected shortfall improves a little on zsRE and worsens a\012little on CounterFact.\012\012The acquisition-and-assay stream totals rise from about six hundred five seconds to seven hundred\012seventy-seven seconds across the two datasets. The separate full harm readout took about five thousand\012seconds for the comparison. I would not describe these results as a clear win or as proof that the\012algorithms are equivalent. This is a one-realization exposed transfer check. It tells us that changing\012this credit rule alone did not produce a compelling new paraphrase benefit in the tested working reader.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
