52 0 obj
<<
/C [ 0 0 0 ] /Contents (Now return to the agent loop. We have examples of successful correction, failures to retrieve, unintended\012changes to nearby facts, and rare losses on ordinary text. Today the investigators choose which of those\012tests to run. An active-inference extension could choose an audit because it expects that observation to\012resolve uncertainty or avoid a costly intervention.\012\012For example, it might have budget for one additional query. It could repeat the teaching question, ask\012a new paraphrase, or test a neighboring fact. Which gives the most useful information about whether the\012stored correction should be used? The concrete proposed comparison is an explicit policy against fixed\012audit schedules, under the same resources, measuring correction utility and harm together.\012\012The feedback from the coupled-entropy work points to a further modeling task: specify the modeled random\012variable, the probability distribution, constraints, weighting and gradients before claiming a coupled\012free-energy objective. Our bounded kappa loss did not do that. The measured mixture is a useful baseline,\012and tail severity gives us a consequence to model, but neither constructs the policy for us. This is a\012post-conference direction, not a promise of another experiment before October ninth.) /Name Comment /QuadPoints [ 1218 675 1235 675 1218 692 1235 692 ] /Rect [ 1218 675 1235 692 ] /Subtype /Text 
  /Type /Annot
>>
endobj
